
.TH LINK 1 "October 2026" "link" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
link \- create a hard link to a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B link
.I FILE1 FILE2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B link
creates a hard link named
.I FILE2
to the existing file
.IR FILE1 .
.PP
The operation uses the
.BR link (2)
system call directly.
Both filenames refer to the same inode and share the same
file contents, permissions, ownership, and timestamps.
.PP
Changes made through either filename are visible through
the other. Removing one link does not delete the file
as long as another hard link remains.
.PP
Hard links cannot normally cross filesystem boundaries
or refer to directories.
The destination must not already exist.
.PP
Unlike
.BR ln ,
this command does not support symbolic links, overwriting
existing files, or creating links to multiple files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option processing, allowing filenames beginning with
a hyphen.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a hard link:
.PP
.RS
.B link original.txt hardlink.txt
.RE
.PP
Both filenames now refer to the same file contents.
.PP
Verify that both filenames share the same inode:
.PP
.RS
.B ls -i original.txt hardlink.txt
.RE
.PP
Create a hard link using filenames beginning with a hyphen:
.PP
.RS
.B link -- -source -destination
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or number of arguments was specified,
or the hard link could not be created.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic functionality conforms to POSIX
.BR link .
The
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
options are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ln (1),
.BR unlink (1),
.BR link (2),
.BR unlink (2),
.BR symlink (2)
